1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,865
A normal A/B test compares two fixed things.

2
00:00:02,865 --> 00:00:07,436
Button colour one against button colour two, each one behaving identically every

3
00:00:07,436 --> 00:00:08,979
single time a user sees it.

4
00:00:08,979 --> 00:00:13,608
Whatever variance shows up in the results comes entirely from the users, not from

5
00:00:13,608 --> 00:00:14,923
the thing being tested.

6
00:00:14,923 --> 00:00:18,638
For an AI feature, that assumption breaks from the first request.

7
00:00:18,638 --> 00:00:23,267
Variant A can produce a genuinely different answer to the identical prompt on two

8
00:00:23,267 --> 00:00:28,010
different occasions, because sampling a model isn't the same operation as rendering

9
00:00:28,010 --> 00:00:28,867
a fixed button.

10
00:00:28,867 --> 00:00:33,439
Now the results carry two sources of variance tangled together: real differences

11
00:00:33,439 --> 00:00:38,011
between users, and the model's own inherent variation, and a standard test can't

12
00:00:38,011 --> 00:00:38,925
tell them apart.

13
00:00:38,925 --> 00:00:41,383
That's not a reason to abandon A/B testing.

14
00:00:41,383 --> 00:00:45,840
It's a reason to run it with the two specific adjustments this lecture covers,

15
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:50,241
because without them the test is answering a question you didn't mean to ask.

16
00:00:50,241 --> 00:00:54,755
This shows up in a specific, recognisable way, on a prompt change to the ticket

17
00:00:54,755 --> 00:00:59,156
summariser, say: a test that looked clearly positive in week one, and quietly

18
00:00:59,156 --> 00:01:03,842
evaporated by week three, with nobody able to say whether the effect was ever real

19
00:01:03,842 --> 00:01:06,928
or whether early noise happened to look like a signal.

20
00:01:06,928 --> 00:01:11,500
That specific pattern is usually the two broken assumptions showing up together.

21
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:16,780
Read the third row as the practical consequence of the first two, because it's the

22
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:19,376
one that actually costs you a wrong decision.

23
00:01:19,376 --> 00:01:24,106
A standard sample-size calculator asks for an effect size and a baseline rate, and

24
00:01:24,106 --> 00:01:25,606
returns a number of users.

25
00:01:25,606 --> 00:01:30,336
That number assumes every unit of noise in the data comes from genuine differences

26
00:01:30,336 --> 00:01:31,202
between people.

27
00:01:31,202 --> 00:01:35,816
Feed it the same inputs for an AI test and it will confidently hand you a sample

28
00:01:35,816 --> 00:01:40,200
size that's too small, because it never accounted for the model's own output

29
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:44,065
variance sitting inside the noise alongside the real user variance.

30
00:01:44,065 --> 00:01:48,334
This is exactly the ground 4.8 already covered, applied to a new question.

31
00:01:48,334 --> 00:01:53,180
That lecture taught you why a probabilistic feature needs more cases to trust a pass

32
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:53,872
rate at all.

33
00:01:53,872 --> 00:01:58,660
This is the same statistics, aimed at comparing two variants instead of scoring one

34
00:01:58,660 --> 00:01:59,871
against a golden set.

35
00:01:59,871 --> 00:02:04,255
Temperature is the specific dial behind the first row, worth naming plainly.

36
00:02:04,255 --> 00:02:08,755
Turn it down and outputs become more consistent, which can shrink this problem

37
00:02:08,755 --> 00:02:13,600
without solving it, because zero temperature still isn't the same guarantee a button

38
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:14,119
click is.

39
00:02:14,119 --> 00:02:18,792
Some providers change model versions underneath a fixed API name without warning,

40
00:02:18,792 --> 00:02:23,580
which reintroduces the same variance from a completely different direction, one you

41
00:02:23,580 --> 00:02:24,907
can't dial down at all.

42
00:02:25,457 --> 00:02:30,060
User-level randomisation matters because a single person bouncing between arms on

43
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:32,902
different requests contaminates both arms at once.

44
00:02:32,902 --> 00:02:37,278
Keep one user in one arm for the test's duration, and you can aggregate their

45
00:02:37,278 --> 00:02:41,939
multiple requests into a more stable read of that arm's true performance, which is

46
00:02:41,939 --> 00:02:45,519
exactly what helps offset the model's own per-request variance.

47
00:02:45,519 --> 00:02:49,782
Pinning the version matters for a reason 9.2 already gave you the tool for.

48
00:02:49,782 --> 00:02:54,329
Every event already carries a prompt version in a properly instrumented feature.

49
00:02:54,329 --> 00:02:58,762
An A/B test simply holds that version fixed on purpose, for both arms, for the

50
00:02:58,762 --> 00:03:03,365
test's full duration, so a mid-test "improvement" doesn't quietly become a third,

51
00:03:03,365 --> 00:03:06,491
unlabelled variant nobody accounted for in the results.

52
00:03:06,491 --> 00:03:10,981
This is also why a test needs a defined end date agreed before it starts, not a

53
00:03:10,981 --> 00:03:14,278
running check that stops the moment the number looks good.

54
00:03:14,278 --> 00:03:18,711
Peeking at results daily and stopping the instant they cross significance is a

55
00:03:18,711 --> 00:03:23,314
well-documented way to manufacture a false positive out of pure noise, and it's a

56
00:03:23,314 --> 00:03:27,691
far easier trap to fall into when the metric is also bouncing around from the

57
00:03:27,691 --> 00:03:30,362
model's own variance on top of everything else.

58
00:03:30,912 --> 00:03:35,656
Say the honest caveat plainly, because underpowering a test in either direction is a

59
00:03:35,656 --> 00:03:37,915
real cost, not an abstract technicality.

60
00:03:37,915 --> 00:03:42,377
Too small a sample and a real improvement looks like noise, so you throw away a

61
00:03:42,377 --> 00:03:43,789
genuinely better version.

62
00:03:43,789 --> 00:03:48,477
Too small a sample the other way, and noise looks like a real effect, so you ship a

63
00:03:48,477 --> 00:03:51,922
change that did nothing, believing the data proved it helped.

64
00:03:51,922 --> 00:03:56,384
Both mistakes trace back to the same root: treating an AI feature's sample-size

65
00:03:56,384 --> 00:03:58,248
maths as identical to a button's.

66
00:03:58,248 --> 00:04:02,710
Neither mistake announces itself at the time, so how would you tell them apart?

67
00:04:02,710 --> 00:04:07,398
Both look, in the moment, like a clean result with a clear recommendation attached.

68
00:04:07,398 --> 00:04:12,142
The only way is to have sized the test properly before it started: a design decision

69
00:04:12,142 --> 00:04:16,773
made in advance, not a judgement call made once the numbers already look a certain

70
00:04:16,773 --> 00:04:16,999
way.

71
00:04:16,999 --> 00:04:21,631
If you don't have a statistician to size the test properly, run it longer than the

72
00:04:21,631 --> 00:04:26,036
calculator suggests, and treat a narrow, borderline result with real suspicion

73
00:04:26,036 --> 00:04:30,102
rather than declaring victory the moment the number crosses a threshold.

74
00:04:30,102 --> 00:04:34,677
That result deserves the same scepticism 4.9 taught you to bring to a single eval

75
00:04:34,677 --> 00:04:38,292
score, not extra credit for having a significance test attached.

76
00:04:38,842 --> 00:04:43,523
That's the standard worth holding every test to, and the example is worth running on

77
00:04:43,523 --> 00:04:46,755
a real test you've already shipped, not just a future one.

78
00:04:46,755 --> 00:04:51,157
Most teams running this check for the first time find at least one violation: a

79
00:04:51,157 --> 00:04:55,615
version that quietly changed mid-test, or randomisation happening at the request

80
00:04:55,615 --> 00:04:57,844
level without anyone deciding it should.

81
00:04:57,844 --> 00:05:01,411
That finding doesn't necessarily mean the past result was wrong.

82
00:05:01,411 --> 00:05:06,036
It means the result deserves less confidence than the dashboard gave it credit for.

83
00:05:06,036 --> 00:05:10,104
Apply the same standard to a test still running, not only a finished one.

84
00:05:10,104 --> 00:05:14,674
If a live test is being randomised per request or running against a prompt someone

85
00:05:14,674 --> 00:05:19,299
quietly edited last week, stop it and fix the setup before trusting a single number

86
00:05:19,299 --> 00:05:20,692
it produces from here on.

87
00:05:21,242 --> 00:05:22,892
One thing before you move on.

88
00:05:22,892 --> 00:05:24,884
Check the randomisation unit first.

89
00:05:24,884 --> 00:05:29,436
If it's per-request rather than per-user, that's the fix to make before the next

90
00:05:29,436 --> 00:05:30,858
test launches, not after.

91
00:05:30,858 --> 00:05:35,638
Then pad the sample size on purpose, and say why, in writing, so whoever reviews the

92
00:05:35,638 --> 00:05:39,109
result later understands it wasn't an arbitrary round number.

93
00:05:39,109 --> 00:05:42,067
Neither of these blocks a test from launching today.

94
00:05:42,067 --> 00:05:46,619
They change what launching responsibly looks like: the same test, with a unit of

95
00:05:46,619 --> 00:05:51,342
randomisation and a version lock decided on purpose instead of left to whatever the

96
00:05:51,342 --> 00:05:52,765
testing tool defaults to.

97
00:05:52,765 --> 00:05:57,374
Next lecture is feedback loops and the data flywheel: what happens to all of this

98
00:05:57,374 --> 00:06:01,869
data once you have it, and the honest limits of using it to improve the feature

99
00:06:01,869 --> 00:06:02,665
automatically.
